[
  {
    "conceptId": "scoping-what-to-build",
    "title": "Scoping your landing page before Claude Code writes a line",
    "hook": "The most expensive hour of this project is the one where you ask Claude Code to “build me a website for my business” and it does. You will get a page. It will look plausible. And you will spend the next three weekends discovering that it takes bookings you do not want, has a blog you will never write, and still does not do the one thing you actually needed — get a message from a stranger into your inbox. The fix costs twenty minutes and no code at all.",
    "landing": "You now have a written boundary for version one: one page, three things on it, one job it must do. Everything else has a name and a place to wait. That sentence is the first thing you will hand to Claude Code.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "s-01",
        "kind": "answer_capsule",
        "text": "Scoping is the short conversation you have before any code exists, where you decide what version one of your page is — and, just as importantly, what it is not. For you that is one page, three things on it, and one job: a message from a stranger reaching your inbox."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-02",
        "kind": "reading",
        "markdown": "Scoping means fixing the **boundary** before anything gets built. Not a plan, not a spec — a boundary. You write down what the first version contains, what it deliberately leaves out, and the one thing that would make you say “that worked”.\n\nIt takes about twenty minutes and it is the only part of this whole project you do entirely without a computer telling you anything.",
        "detail": "The reason it comes first is not tidiness. An AI assistant will happily build whatever you describe, at speed, and it has no opinion about whether you needed it. That is a genuinely new problem: the old constraint on building the wrong thing was that building anything was slow and expensive, so you thought first. That constraint is gone. Scoping is what you put in its place — deliberately, because nothing else will do it for you."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-03",
        "kind": "svg_diagram",
        "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 376\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"On the left, a dashed cloud labelled everything you could build, holding seven ideas. On the right, a small solid box labelled version one, holding three. An arrow between them is labelled twenty minutes of deciding.\">\n  <text x=\"152\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.6\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.62\">EVERYTHING YOU COULD BUILD</text>\n  <g transform=\"translate(0,36)\">\n    <g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\">\n      <path d=\"M18 92 C18 46 66 30 104 44 C126 12 190 10 208 42 C258 26 300 52 296 92 C330 116 326 176 292 190 C300 236 248 268 208 250 C182 284 112 282 96 248 C46 258 10 220 26 180 C-2 156 -2 112 18 92 Z\" stroke-dasharray=\"9 8\"/>\n    </g>\n    <g font-size=\"15\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.72\">\n      <text x=\"60\" y=\"96\">a blog</text>\n      <text x=\"164\" y=\"84\">online booking</text>\n      <text x=\"52\" y=\"146\">a shop</text>\n      <text x=\"170\" y=\"150\">a newsletter</text>\n      <text x=\"70\" y=\"200\">reviews</text>\n      <text x=\"168\" y=\"216\">a photo gallery</text>\n      <text x=\"96\" y=\"248\">live chat</text>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n\n  <g stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M352 190 h56\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n    <path d=\"M396 178 l14 12 l-14 12\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"380\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.2\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.62\">20 MINUTES</text>\n  <text x=\"380\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.2\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.62\">OF DECIDING</text>\n\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.6\" fill=\"currentColor\">VERSION ONE</text>\n  <rect x=\"462\" y=\"108\" width=\"280\" height=\"172\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.14\"/>\n  <rect x=\"456\" y=\"102\" width=\"280\" height=\"172\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"currentColor\">\n    <text x=\"504\" y=\"146\">who I am, in my words</text>\n    <text x=\"504\" y=\"186\">six photos of my work</text>\n    <text x=\"504\" y=\"226\">a form that reaches my inbox</text>\n  </g>\n  <g stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" fill=\"none\">\n    <path d=\"M476 140 l7 7 l12 -14\"/>\n    <path d=\"M476 180 l7 7 l12 -14\"/>\n    <path d=\"M476 220 l7 7 l12 -14\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.72\">Everything on the left still exists. It is just waiting.</text>\n</svg>",
        "caption": "Scoping is the line between the two shapes. Nothing is thrown away — the left-hand side is a list you keep."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-04",
        "kind": "callout",
        "tone": "jargon",
        "markdown": "**Scope** is just “what is in and what is out”. When somebody says a project has “scope creep”, they mean things kept quietly getting added until nobody could finish. You will hear the word a lot. It means nothing more complicated than that."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-05",
        "kind": "reading",
        "markdown": "Here is why this matters for **your** page specifically. You said the thing that hurts is that people can only find you by word of mouth. That means version one has exactly one job: somebody who has never met you lands on your page, believes you are real, and gets a message to you without picking up the phone.\n\nA booking system does not do that. A blog does not do that. A newsletter does not do that. They are all good ideas later, and every one of them is a week you are not live."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-06",
        "kind": "quiz",
        "question": "You have an afternoon. Which of these actually belongs in version one of your page?",
        "variant": {
          "type": "mcq",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "A photo of you and three of your work, with a sentence saying what you do",
              "correct": true,
              "feedback": "Yes. A stranger has to believe you are a real person who does this. Photos and one honest sentence do that faster than anything else on the page."
            },
            {
              "text": "A contact form that sends the message to your inbox",
              "correct": true,
              "feedback": "Yes — this is the one job. If everything else on the page were missing and this worked, version one would still have paid for itself."
            },
            {
              "text": "A calendar where customers pick a slot and book themselves in",
              "correct": false,
              "feedback": "Not yet, and it is the most tempting one. Booking means payments, cancellations, your real availability and somebody being let down when it goes wrong. A form that says “when suits you?” gets you the same customer this month."
            },
            {
              "text": "A blog so the page shows up on Google",
              "correct": false,
              "feedback": "Later, honestly. A blog only works if you write it every week for months. An empty blog with one post from last March actively makes a business look abandoned."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s-07",
        "kind": "callout",
        "tone": "reassurance",
        "markdown": "Cutting something in this conversation is not giving up on it. It is giving it a date. “Not in version one” and “never” are completely different sentences, and the list you park today is the thing you build next month — on a site that is already earning its keep."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-08",
        "kind": "reading",
        "markdown": "Now the part that is specific to building with AI. You do not scope **at** Claude Code, you scope **with** it — and then you make it repeat the boundary back to you before it touches anything.\n\nThe move is simple: describe the business, describe the one job, and ask it to tell you what it thinks version one should contain. Then argue with the answer. It will suggest more than you need, every time, because more is what it has read the most of."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-09",
        "kind": "code_block",
        "language": "text",
        "code": "I run a small business on my own and I want a simple one-page website.\n\nThe one job of version one: somebody who has never met me lands on the\npage, believes I'm real, and sends me a message without phoning.\n\nI have: some photos of my work, and about a paragraph about what I do.\nI have never built anything before.\n\nBefore writing any code, tell me:\n  1. what you think version one should contain, and nothing more\n  2. what you'd deliberately leave out, and why\n  3. the one assumption you're making that, if it's wrong,\n     would change the whole approach\n\nDon't write any code yet. I want to agree the boundary first.",
        "explanation": "Paste this, adjusted to your own business, as the very first thing you say. The three numbered questions are the whole technique: the first two give you a boundary, and the third is the one people skip. It is asking the assistant to name what it has quietly assumed — that you want the form to email you rather than store messages somewhere, say — while changing course is still free."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-10",
        "kind": "activity",
        "title": "Write your boundary down, in one page of plain words",
        "steps": [
          "Open a plain note — paper is completely fine for this one.",
          "Write the one job of version one as a single sentence, starting “Somebody who has never met me…”.",
          "Under it, list what is on the page. Stop when you reach four things.",
          "Under that, write “Not yet:” and list everything you just thought of and cut. Give the list a heading and keep it.",
          "Read the one-job sentence out loud. If it contains the word “and” twice, you still have two projects."
        ],
        "capturesProjectFact": {
          "field": "versionOneBoundary",
          "prompt": "What is the one job of version one, in one sentence?"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "s-11",
        "kind": "callout",
        "tone": "warning",
        "markdown": "**The nasty surprise, and it is not a technical one.** The assistant will never tell you that you are building too much. It has no stake in you finishing. It will accept “actually, can it also do bookings?” with the same enthusiasm it accepted everything else, and three weekends later you will have a half-built booking system and no live website. Nobody will stop that except you, and the boundary you wrote today is how you do it."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-12",
        "kind": "reading",
        "markdown": "One more thing to watch for, and it is the expensive kind because it is hard to undo later: **the assumption nobody says out loud.**\n\nFor your page the big one is what happens to a message when somebody presses Send. Email it to you and never store it? Keep every enquiry somewhere you can look back at? Those are different builds, and the second one is much bigger. Decide it now, in a sentence, while the answer is still free.",
        "detail": "The general shape of this: some decisions are cheap to reverse and some are not. Changing a colour, moving a photo, rewording a heading — free, forever. Where your data lives, whether you need somewhere for it to live at all, what your page is actually called on the internet — those get more expensive every week you leave them.\n\nYou do not have to get the expensive ones right. You have to get them *decided*, on purpose, instead of discovering that the assistant decided for you in week one and you have been building on it since."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-13",
        "kind": "capture",
        "prompt": "When somebody presses Send on your form, what should happen to that message?",
        "field": "whatHappensToAMessage",
        "hint": "In your own words. “It should just land in my email” is a complete and very good answer."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-14",
        "kind": "artifact_challenge",
        "title": "One page that says what version one is",
        "brief": "Write your scope note — half a page, plain words, no jargon and no code. It should say: the one job of version one; what is on the page; what you have deliberately left for later; and the one assumption that would change everything if it turned out to be wrong.\n\nWrite it as though you were handing it to somebody else to build. In a sense you are: this is the first thing you will give Claude Code, and it is what you will hold it to when it suggests something shiny in week two.",
        "rubric": [
          "States the one job of version one as a single sentence about a real person doing a real thing",
          "Lists what is on the page, and the list stops at four things or fewer",
          "Has a “not yet” list with at least two things on it that you genuinely wanted",
          "Names at least one assumption that would change the approach if it were wrong",
          "Contains no technical words that you could not explain to a friend"
        ],
        "acceptedFormats": [
          "text",
          "markdown"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "s-15",
        "kind": "svg_diagram",
        "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 322\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two timelines. Building everything first reaches week twelve without going live. Building version one first goes live in week two, and the other pieces still get added afterwards.\">\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.5\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.62\">IF YOU BUILD EVERYTHING FIRST</text>\n  <g stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\">\n    <rect x=\"20\" y=\"48\" width=\"132\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"156\" y=\"48\" width=\"132\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"292\" y=\"48\" width=\"132\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"428\" y=\"48\" width=\"132\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"564\" y=\"48\" width=\"132\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.72\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <text x=\"86\" y=\"75\">the page</text>\n    <text x=\"222\" y=\"75\">booking</text>\n    <text x=\"358\" y=\"75\">the shop</text>\n    <text x=\"494\" y=\"75\">a gallery</text>\n    <text x=\"630\" y=\"75\">the blog</text>\n  </g>\n  <g stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\" opacity=\"0.55\"><circle cx=\"716\" cy=\"69\" r=\"11\"/></g>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.72\">Week 12. Nobody outside your kitchen has seen any of it.</text>\n\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"174\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.5\" fill=\"currentColor\">IF YOU BUILD VERSION ONE FIRST</text>\n  <rect x=\"26\" y=\"198\" width=\"132\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.14\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"192\" width=\"132\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n  <text x=\"86\" y=\"219\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the page</text>\n  <circle cx=\"176\" cy=\"213\" r=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n  <text x=\"198\" y=\"218\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"currentColor\">LIVE — WEEK 2</text>\n  <g stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 7\" opacity=\"0.6\">\n    <rect x=\"356\" y=\"192\" width=\"122\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"490\" y=\"192\" width=\"122\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\"/>\n    <rect x=\"624\" y=\"192\" width=\"112\" height=\"42\" rx=\"3\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.6\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <text x=\"417\" y=\"218\">booking, wk 5</text>\n    <text x=\"551\" y=\"218\">gallery, wk 8</text>\n    <text x=\"680\" y=\"218\">the rest</text>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"266\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.72\">Week 2. A stranger can find you and send you a message.</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"302\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.72\">Nothing on the top row has been cancelled. It has just moved behind the thing that pays for it.</text>\n</svg>",
        "caption": "The same seven ideas, in two different orders. Only one of them has a customer in it."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-16",
        "kind": "curated_video",
        "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eDBYYLEsECU",
        "startSeconds": 0,
        "endSeconds": 420,
        "whyItMatters": "DataCamp walking through a first session with Claude Code — what the screen looks like, what you type, what it does back. Worth seven minutes before you open it yourself, because the single most disorienting thing about the first session is not knowing what is supposed to happen. It is pitched at people who already write code, so it will move past you in places. Stop when you have the shape of it; you are not behind."
      },
      {
        "id": "s-17",
        "kind": "svg_diagram",
        "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 760 300\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A line running from cheap to change on the left to expensive the longer you leave it on the right. Colours, wording and photo order sit on the left. What the page is called, where messages go, and whether you need somewhere to keep them sit on the right.\">\n  <g stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">\n    <path d=\"M40 168 h676\"/>\n    <path d=\"M700 156 l16 12 l-16 12\"/>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"34\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.5\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.62\">CHEAP TO CHANGE, FOREVER</text>\n  <text x=\"716\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" letter-spacing=\"1.5\" fill=\"currentColor\">EXPENSIVE, AND MORE SO EVERY WEEK</text>\n\n  <g fill=\"currentColor\">\n    <circle cx=\"86\" cy=\"168\" r=\"5\" opacity=\"0.45\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"214\" cy=\"168\" r=\"6\" opacity=\"0.55\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"342\" cy=\"168\" r=\"7\" opacity=\"0.65\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"470\" cy=\"168\" r=\"9\" opacity=\"0.8\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"588\" cy=\"168\" r=\"11\" opacity=\"0.9\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"690\" cy=\"168\" r=\"13\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" opacity=\"0.4\">\n    <path d=\"M86 128 v28\"/><path d=\"M342 128 v26\"/><path d=\"M588 128 v20\"/>\n    <path d=\"M214 180 v28\"/><path d=\"M470 180 v22\"/><path d=\"M690 180 v18\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n    <text x=\"86\" y=\"120\" opacity=\"0.72\">the colours</text>\n    <text x=\"342\" y=\"120\" opacity=\"0.72\">which photos</text>\n    <text x=\"588\" y=\"120\">where a message goes</text>\n    <text x=\"214\" y=\"226\" opacity=\"0.72\">the wording</text>\n    <text x=\"470\" y=\"226\">what your page is called</text>\n    <text x=\"690\" y=\"226\">whether you need</text>\n    <text x=\"690\" y=\"244\">somewhere to keep them</text>\n  </g>\n  <text x=\"380\" y=\"288\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\" opacity=\"0.72\">You do not have to get the right-hand end right. You have to get it DECIDED, on purpose.</text>\n</svg>",
        "caption": "Some decisions stay free. Others get more expensive every week you leave them unmade."
      }
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "step-1",
        "title": "What this conversation actually is",
        "goal": "You can say what scoping is, and why it comes before anything gets built.",
        "sectionIds": [
